@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — dissolution named as the death of the creature
#
# One node. It belongs to this directory and not to a tradition because the
# text was attributed by its own author to a second-century teacher who did
# not write it, and is still read under that attribution.
#

pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Distinctiveness as the striving of the creature against dissolution"@en, "Die Unterschiedenheit als Streben des Geschöpfs gegen die Auflösung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a creature which ceases to distinguish falls into the undifferentiated fullness, ceases to be a creature and is given over to dissolution in nothingness, so that its natural striving runs towards distinctiveness against a primeval and perilous sameness; and that the striving is nevertheless not after difference but after the creature's own being."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because it is the only position located so far that names both ends of the axis as a loss and then states a third movement. Dissolution into the fullness is called the death of the creature outright, which rules out the unitive end; and the striving is expressly not after difference as it is thought, which rules out the separative one. What remains is a striving after one's own being, and the source gives it the name principium individuationis."@en ;
    skos:note "The god named above the god of the pairs of opposites belongs to the same argument and not to a separate doctrine: the qualities of the fullness are given as pairs, and what is set above them unites the full and the empty. A reading that takes this as a third party in a pantheon takes the figure for the argument."@en ;
    skos:note "The attribution is the reason this is an origination and not a witness of gnostic transmission. The author printed the text under the name of a second-century teacher of Alexandria who did not write it, and it continues to be read and cited as gnostic teaching. pm:circulatesAs records that, and the node makes no judgement about the author's own reading of the ancient material."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. G. Jung" ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A gnostic text of the second century, printed under the name of Basilides of Alexandria and read as ancient teaching rather than as a work written in 1916." ;
    dcterms:date "1916" ;
    dcterms:source "C. G. Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos, Sermo I (English translation of the 1916 private printing, Gnostic Society Library electronic edition)" .
